1. Test Plan

Definition:
A Test Plan is a detailed document that describes what to test, how to test, when to test, and who will test.

Key Points:

Project-specific (changes for every project)
Created by: Test Manager / QA Lead
Covers:
Scope
Objectives
Resources
Schedule
Risks
Test environment

Example:
For a banking app:

Test login, fund transfer, account balance
Assign testers
Define timelines

🔹 2. Test Strategy

Definition:
A Test Strategy is a high-level document that defines the overall testing approach.

Key Points:

Organization-level or long-term
Mostly static (rarely changes)
Created by: Senior QA / Management
Covers:
Testing types (functional, regression, performance)
Tools to use (Selenium, JMeter)
Standards & guidelines

Example:

Automation for regression
Manual testing for UI
Use Agile methodology

🔹 3. Test Scenario

Definition:
A Test Scenario is a high-level test condition that defines what functionality to test.

Key Points:

Derived from requirements
No detailed steps
Covers user actions

Example:

Verify login functionality
Verify password reset
Verify checkout process
🔥 Key Differences (Quick Table)
Aspect	Test Plan 📝	Test Strategy 📊	Test Scenario 🎯
Level	Detailed	High-level	Very high-level
Purpose	Execution planning	Testing approach	What to test
Scope	Project-specific	Organization-wide	Feature-specific
Created By	QA Lead / Manager	Senior Management	QA/Testers
Changes	Frequently updated	Rarely changes	Can change often
Includes Steps?	Yes (indirectly via cases)	No	No
💡 Simple Way to Remember
Test Strategy = "How we test"
Test Plan = "What, when, who tests"
Test Scenario = "What to test (high-level)"

