🔹 1. BRS (Business Requirement Specification)

What it is:

High-level business needs (client perspective)

Example:

User should be able to transfer money
User should login securely

👉 No technical details

🔹 2. SRS (Software Requirement Specification)

What it is:

Detailed functional + technical requirements (system perspective)

Includes:

Functional requirements
Non-functional (performance, security)
UI behavior
API details

👉 This is the main input for QA

🔹 3. Test Strategy (High-Level Approach)

Derived From: SRS + Organization standards

Defines:

Testing types (functional, regression, automation)
Tools (Selenium, TestNG)
Environments
Risk handling

👉 Stable document (rarely changes)

🔹 4. Test Plan (Project-Level Planning)

Derived From: SRS + Test Strategy

Defines:

Scope (what to test / not test)
Timeline
Resources
Roles & responsibilities
Entry/Exit criteria

👉 Project-specific and dynamic

🔹 5. Test Scenarios (High-Level Conditions)

Derived From: SRS

Examples:

Verify login functionality
Verify fund transfer
Verify logout

👉 No steps, just coverage points

🔹 6. Test Cases (Detailed Steps)

Derived From: Test Scenarios

Includes:

Preconditions
Test steps
Expected results
Actual results

Example:

Enter username/password → Click login → Verify dashboard

🔹 7. Test Data

What it is:

Input values for testing

Examples:

Valid login → user123 / pass123
Invalid login → wrong password

🔹 8. Test Execution

What happens:

Run test cases
Mark:
Pass ✅
Fail ❌

🔹 9. Defect/Bug Logging

When: Test case fails

Logged in tools:
JIRA, Bugzilla
Includes:

Steps to reproduce
Severity / Priority
Screenshots

🔹 10. Test Reporting

Final output:

Test summary report
Metrics:
Passed/Failed cases
Defect count
Coverage
